    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. the analogies help. so when you talk about those \"sneaky\" or \"low-level\" attackers who try to bypass things, are we talking about organized groups or just people looking for easy holes? i'm trying to get a feel for what these threats actually behave like so i can explain the urgency to my partners.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"sneaky\" attackers you're describing can range from a disorganized, opportunistic individual looking for an easy target to a highly organized, well-funded group. The low-level threat is often the one that causes the most widespread, annoying disruption, like a rogue intern trying to steal a few client files for a quick score. These are the ones who might try to exploit a known, unpatched vulnerability in a web portal to quietly slip in.\n\nHowever, the more sophisticated threats—the organized groups—are often more patient and methodical, looking for the deepest, most valuable holes. They aren't just after a quick smash-and-grab; they are methodically mapping out the firm's defenses, like a patient scout before a major raid.\n\nWhen explaining this to your partners, you can frame it as a spectrum: the low-hanging fruit is easy for both amateurs and professionals, but the true risk comes from the persistent, dedicated actors who will keep trying until they find a weakness. The Yubikey helps stop the *easy* attempts, which is where most of the noise comes from.",
      "source": "target",
